    "long_instructions": "You’re building a nearest-neighbor image classifier, but it needs data to help it make predictions.\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n\r\n1. Replace the `training_images` list with a list of dictionaries representing your training data. The list should have one dictionary for each training image in your dataset.\r\n\r\n    a. For each image, replace the `category` value (“Category A” or “Category B”) with the name for the image’s category.\r\n\r\n   \r\n    b. For each image, replace the `feature1`, `feature2`, and `feature3` keys with names for your three features. Replace each value with `True` or `False` based on your training data.\r\n\r\n2. Replace the `test_image` with a dictionary representing the three features from any one of your test images.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nGive each of your feature keys names that represent what they mean. You can pick any names you want, but be consistent across all your data! For example, a training image for telling apart oceans and rivers might look like:\r\n\r\n```python\r\n{\"category\": \"Ocean\", \"white_waves\": True, \"trees\": False, \"touch_sky\": True}\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou can copy/paste an entry in the `training_images` list to save typing time when creating a new image!",
